# Credit Card Fraud Detection: SVM vs Decision Tree

## Overview
This project compares a Decision Tree Classifier and a Linear SVM (Support Vector Machine) on a highly imbalanced credit card fraud dataset, evaluated using ROC-AUC. It also studies how feature selection affects each model.

## Dataset
IBM Credit Card Fraud Dataset (creditcard.csv)

## Technologies
- Python
- Pandas
- Scikit-learn
- Matplotlib

## Workflow
1. Load dataset
2. Class imbalance visualization
3. Correlation analysis with target (`Class`)
4. Feature standardization and L1 normalization
5. Train/Test split
6. Class imbalance handling via sample weights
7. Decision Tree training and evaluation (max_depth=4)
8. Linear SVM training and evaluation (class_weight='balanced')
9. Top 6 correlated feature selection
10. Model re-evaluation on reduced feature set (Q3/Q4)
11. Comparative inference (Q5)

## Evaluation Metrics
- ROC-AUC Score

## Results
- Full feature set: both models score well, with feature scale/imbalance handling in place.
- Reduced feature set (top 6 features): Decision Tree ROC-AUC increases (benefits from feature selection); SVM ROC-AUC decreases (needs higher dimensionality to build an effective hyperplane).
- Inference: Decision Trees favor a small, informative feature set; SVMs favor higher-dimensional feature spaces.
